## A table for data

In the lesson on 1996 you saw tables stand in for a grid. But a table was invented for something else: data that has rows and columns. A timetable, prices, a comparison of plans, a list of documents with dates. In such a table a cell gets its meaning from its row and its column: “from $000” means a price because it sits in the Price column, in the Survey row.

A good test: take away the visible grid. Does the neighborly arrangement still carry meaning? For a menu on the left and text on the right it doesn’t: that’s layout. For a price and a service it does: that’s a table.

## What a table is made of

```html
<table>
  <caption>What is included and what it costs</caption>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th scope="col">Item</th>
      <th scope="col">What is included</th>
      <th scope="col">Price</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <th scope="row">Consultation</th>
      <td>An hour on your documents</td>
      <td>$000</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
```

- `<caption>` is the title: what the table is about. It comes first;
- `<thead>` and `<tbody>` are the head and the body. There is also `<tfoot>` for totals;
- `<tr>` is a row;
- `<th>` is a header cell, and `<td>` is a cell of data;
- `scope="col"` says the header applies to a column, and `scope="row"` that it applies to a row.

You can merge cells (`colspan`, `rowspan`), but complicated tables are painful for people who use a screen reader. If you can’t do without merging, it may really be two tables.

## What a screen reader says

It’s `th` and `scope` that make a table make sense to someone listening to it. When the reading cursor lands on a cell, the software names the column and row headers along with it. Switch the table type in the diagram and pick cells.

<TableReader />

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: why use th when you could just make the cell bold?</summary>

Bold type is seen only by eyes. A th with a scope tells software that this is a header, and what it applies to.

</details>

## Table or cards

Not everything that looks like a list should be a table. Choose like this:

- **a table** is for when the data is uniform and needs comparing: prices by plan, a timetable by day;
- **cards or a list** are for when the items stand alone and each has its own text: the description of a service, a review, a member of staff.

That’s why on our page the services live both in cards (the story) and in a table of prices (the comparison). It isn’t duplication: they are two ways of reading the same thing.

Tables and phones don’t get on well, because the columns don’t fit. The cure is to wrap the table in a block that scrolls sideways, not to shrink the type or turn the table into a picture. You’ll learn exactly how in the CSS part.

## Practice

Add a table of prices or terms to the page. Your data is already there in the cards; carry the name, description and price over from them. If your project has no prices (a club, an event, a subscription service), make an “Opening hours” or “Timetable” table: columns for “Day” and “Time” are real data too.

This version stays in the 2005 era: data tables existed then, and people were already writing `th` and `caption` in them, though many forgot.

## What was wrong with 2005

Data tables and layout tables were mixed up beyond telling apart: a page might have ten of them. Anyone who wanted a real table often fell into a trap: it, too, got nested inside a layout cell. And `th` with `scope` was rare in those days: they were used by those who thought about accessibility, and there were few such authors. Today the table in its true sense is alive, and layout with tables is not.
